hi this is dr. Gregory Sadler I'm a  professor of philosophy and the  president and founder of an educational  consulting company called reason IO  where we put philosophy into practice  I've studied in taught philosophy for  over 20 years and I find that many  people run into difficulties reading  classic philosophical texts sometimes  it's the way things are said or how the  text is structured but the concepts  themselves are not always that  complicated and that's where I come in  to help students and lifelong learners  I've been producing longer lecture  videos and posting them to youtube many  viewers say they find them useful what  you're currently watching is part of a  new series of shorter videos each of  them focused on one core concept from an  important philosophical text I hope you  find it useful as well  towards the end of section 2 of his  groundwork of the metaphysics of morals  among you'll can spend some time talking  about other principles of morality all  of which ultimately come back to what he  calls heteronomy of the will and we'll  talk about that in a moment but you  notice that on the board here we've got  a number of different principles or  essentially grounds for moral theories  set out for them  and what we're going to do is talk about  each of them first we're going to talk  about what each of them is what it is  that they say morality consists in then  we'll talk about Kant's criticisms of  each of these and why they end up being  a type of heteronomy and then we'll  finish by talking about what sort of  ordering Kant gives to them he says that  that they're all bad but some of them  are less bad or at least better in some  respects than the other ones so what is  heteronomy to begin with heteronomy is  opposed to autonomy which is when the  will gives itself its own determination  and for Kant determination in terms of  morality that is in terms of our duties  and what we ought to do and what the  good is all these sorts of things he  conceives of this in terms of laws or  rules norms if you if you like so to be  autonomous means not only to choose for  yourself or to determine yourself but  it's to actually give yourself laws to  give yourself the principles the guiding  norms by which you are going to make  moral decisions and engage in moral  action so what does heteronomy that well  in heteronomy instead of the will  deciding for itself or the person  through their will deciding for  themselves instead you have some sort of  object and it's the objects as he says  relation to the will providing it with  some sort of inducement some sort of  interest appealing to for example the  Wills liking this  particular thing or being drawn by  desire or by inclination that is what  determines the will so quite literally  it's you know law or rule by by another  heteros means other and so Kant says  there's four main ways in which we can  distinguish the moral principles by  which a person would have a heteronomous  will he says that two of these are what  he calls empirical that is based on our  experience there that based on some some  things that we encounter in experience  and then draw from from them and then  two of them are what he calls rational  which means that they appeal to things  that go beyond the realm of our  experience and they're not for that that  matter necessarily false or anything  like that but they are not found solely  within our experience so what are these  on the side of the empirical we have he  first says individual or it can be  translated sometimes as private or  personal eigen in happiness and Kant has  a particular conception of happiness  happiness is the satisfaction of all of  our inclination and desires that is what  he thinks happiness consists in so that  is one thing what would that be as a  moral theory or as a principle would say  well you should do what is actually  going to make you happy so you should  behave in a good way because that's  actually going to make you happy they'll  make you you know successful or people  will like you or you won't get in  trouble so that's that's that theory  then Conte says there's also moral  feeling right gufu in in german a very  similar sense but this is what we would  talk about in terms of feeling  compassion or sympathy or to use a word  that people love to throw around these  days empathy and there were indeed some  moral theories that were basing  themselves at least in part on the  notion of sympathy Hume and  Adam Smith talked about this  jean-jacques rousseau thinks that this  plays an important role in morality so  you have some representatives of this  then on the other side of the rationale  what he calls the principle of  perfection we're dealing with something  that's a bit more metaphysical or a bit  more abstract and he talks first about  the principle of perfection full kommen  height in German and here we would think  about many of what we typically call  today virtue ethics you know ethics of  personal realization of becoming the  best you any of those sorts of things  those would be what he's calling this  principle of perfection and the idea is  that there's some sort of you know  either  it's human nature as such or it's and  you know the reality is such and you  want to realize the the you know full  potential of it and come to the fully  developed State for the kind of being  that we are that is human being then the  fourth we have a Divine Will or command  something to coming to us from God or  the gods or however else we conceive of  as the divine and it tells us what we  ought to do why is this placed under the  principle of perfection well because  it's understood as coming from a  divinely perfect will in this case we're  trying to have a divinely perfect will  in this case there's some sort of  divinely perfect will out there that  were somehow tapping into or fortunate  enough to receive a revelation by now  all of these are problematic according  to